// An instant, and the offset it was written with.
//
// ## The offset that was lost, patched, probed, and lost again
//
// `packages/datetime`'s `Parsed` is `(bool ok, i64 millis, i32 offsetMin, bool offsetKnown)`, and
// the fourth field's doc records what happened:
//
// > RFC 3339 §4.3 gives `-00:00` a meaning of its own: the instant is known and the local offset is
// > *not*. `Z` and `+00:00` both assert an offset of zero. **All three arrived here as
// > `offsetMin == 0` and were indistinguishable**, so the one piece of information this field exists
// > to preserve — the offset as written, because it cannot be recovered from `millis` — was exactly
// > the piece lost.
//
// So a `bool` was added beside the `i32` to carry a third case the `i32` could not hold. That is the
// patch, and the chain does not stop there — see the foot of this file, where it runs through four
// more layers and ends in a probe export that nothing called for long enough to make a coverage
// number wrong.
//
// Three cases is what the RFC has, so three arms is what this is.

/**
 * What a timestamp said about its own offset.
 *
 * `At(0)` and `Utc` are the same instant and different assertions — `+00:00` says *the local offset
 * is zero* and `Z` says *this is UTC* — and `Unknown` is `-00:00`, which says *the instant is known
 * and the offset is not*. The RFC distinguishes all three and only one type can.
 */
export enum Offset {
  /** `Z` or `z`. */
  Utc,
  /** `-00:00`, and the only negative zero in the format. */
  Unknown,
  /** Any explicit numeric offset, including `+00:00`. */
  At(i32 minutes),

  /** Minutes east of UTC, for the arithmetic. `Unknown` contributes nothing, which is the point. */
  i32 minutes(const this) {
    match (this) { At(m): { return m; } else: { return 0; } }
  }
}

/**
 * An instant, and how it was written.
 *
 * `millis` is the whole of the instant; `offset` is not recoverable from it and is kept for a caller
 * that wants to render a timestamp the way it arrived. Formatting to UTC ignores it, which is why
 * the shipped `format` takes a bare `i64` and is right to.
 */
export struct Timestamp {
  i64 millis;
  Offset offset;
}

// ## The chain, because one lost distinction produced five things
//
// Worth writing out, because each link looks reasonable and the last one is a coverage bug:
//
// 1. **`i32 offsetMin` cannot hold three cases**, so `-00:00` and `+00:00` were the same value.
// 2. **`bool offsetKnown` was added** beside it — a second field to carry the third case, with a
//    nine-line comment explaining why it exists.
// 3. **A struct cannot cross the host boundary**, so `packages/datetime/test/probe.wac` exports one
//    function per field: `accepts`, `parseMillis`, `parseOffset`, `parseOffsetKnown`.
// 4. **Each of those has to invent a value for a failed parse.** `parseMillis` and `parseOffset`
//    answer `0` — *"return p.ok ? p.millis : 0"* — which for `parseOffset` is the same `0` that
//    `+00:00` gives. The distinction of step 1, reintroduced one layer out.
// 5. **`parseOffsetKnown` answers an `i32`** — `-1` for *did not parse*, `1` and `0` for the bool —
//    because a `bool` had nowhere to put the failure. A two-valued answer became three-valued with
//    a sentinel, to carry a failure that a `Result` would have carried instead.
//
// And then the last one: **nothing called it.** The README:
//
// > `wac task coverage:datetime` reports 100% — and did not when that line was last checked. It said
// > 92%, because `cov.ts` and `test/datetime.test.ts` are two exercises of the same code and had
// > drifted apart in both directions … and **neither of them ever called `parseOffsetKnown`** — a
// > probe export written for GitHub wac-mono#15, with a nine-line rationale on the field it reports,
// > called by nothing.
//
// Every link is a local decision that is hard to argue with. What the chain shows is that **a
// distinction lost at the bottom is re-lost at every layer that tries to carry it**, and the cost is
// paid in fields, exports, sentinels and eventually in a number that was wrong for months.
//
// `Offset` above removes links 1 and 2. Links 3 to 5 are the host boundary and are not this file's
// to fix — `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has what crossing it costs, since `@/packages/bignum` met the
// same wall from the other side and `@/packages/abi`'s answer is positional for the same reason.
